    "format": "%w(40,0,4)%C(always,blue)%b%Creset",
    "rev": "2bd351db33f7b82f3ac16dd81f1d79b3fdf19541",
    "color": true,
    "rc": 0,
    "out": "\u001b[34mThe previous scanner conflated lexing\n    and evaluation, which made it\n    impossible to attribute an output\n    byte range back to the placeholder\n    that produced it. That attribution\n    is load-bearing for reverse\n    propagation, so the scanner is now\n    split into two phases.\n\n    Phase one produces a span table:\n    each entry records (start, end,\n    kind, source offset). Phase two\n    evaluates spans against a commit\n    record. Because phase one is\n    independent of commit data, the span\n    table can be computed once per \n    template and reused across every\n    previewed commit.\n\n    Unicode note: offsets are byte\n    offsets, not codepoint offsets,\n    because git's padding directives\n    count display columns while\n    truncation counts bytes. Mixing the\n    two silently corrupts CJK and emoji\n    output. Test vectors cover\n    日本語テキスト and 🌍 explicitly.\n\n    BREAKING CHANGE: TokenScanner.next()\n    no longer returns evaluated text.\n\n    Refs: #500, #501 Reviewed-by: Grace\n    Hopper <grace@example.org> \n    Signed-off-by: Ada Lovelace\n    <ada@example.org>\n    \u001b[m",
    "err": ""
  },
